cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark, the reference sets or the derived data, please cite it as below."
title: "synthscore: a makeability benchmark for synthesizability prediction"
abstract: >-
  Synthesizability scores gate most generative and virtual-screening workflows
  at a published global cutoff, and what those cutoffs discard had not been
  measured, because measuring it requires molecules whose route length is known
  independently of any score. This release supplies six reference sets ordered
  by what is independently known about each, from purchasable compounds through
  make-on-demand chemistry of stated step count to sets with no record of
  synthesis, together with the scorer panel and the code that produced the
  results. Scoring them inverts the expected relationship: a 10,000-member
  tetrahydroquinoline library demonstrated to be one step from commercial
  building blocks is rejected by RAscore at its published cutoff in 9,907 of
  10,000 cases, while a fragment-recombination library that has never been made
  scores like purchasable compounds. Retrosynthesis search reproduces the
  inversion rather than correcting it, finding complete routes for 40% of
  never-made recombination products against 1 of 50 one-step compounds. The
  scores rank molecules by resemblance to historical catalogue chemistry rather
  than by synthetic accessibility, and because the field's current easy versus
  hard benchmark varies makeability and catalogue resemblance together, a score
  capturing only the second would appear to succeed on it. The six sets populate
  the cells that comparison omits. Per-target-class operating points are
  included but are documented as a remedy that does not work.
authors:
  - family-names: "Tiwari"
    given-names: "Harshita"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-10-23"
